In 2026, depression is thought of as something to be avoided, especially in the United States. I instead depressionmax and get super depressed and dive into the depression, not try to swim out.

But much of depression are serious signals your soul is trying to tell you about whether your life is working for you. As a society, we're too eager to ignore those signals.

By avoidancemaxxing (the American default), you ignore key signals about what matters to you. And that's a great way to live a shit life.

So here I suggest depressionmaxxing, and getting fucked up and sad. You might learn a thing or two… and it might change your life.

## What is Depression?

In medicine, depression is a cluster of symptoms that characterize malaise and reduction in mood [1].

In culture it typically means something like low-mood, or an inability to enjoy life.

In both of these definitions, the focus on depression is focused entirely on the person whose actions and experience is depressive.

## Depression is Relational

The treatment of depressive symptoms as individualistic is one of the big bugs in modern mental health.

Most westerners think of depression in terms that square exclusively on the depressed person:

> He was depressed and can't get out of bed

I suggest treating depression as relational:

> He was in a situation and state of mind such that his actions were depressive

In other words:

1. **individualistic model** (modern medicine): depression is something wrong with the person
1. **relational model** (common sense model): depression is the relationship between a person and the environment they experience

In the individualistic model, we are inclined to focus on how to change or fix the person suffering. For example, modern American therapy would have you learn to cope with your situation, even if it's fucked up.

By thinking relationally, we can inspect the actual causes of depression, and not pathologize totally reasonable depressive episodes and phases. A good, sensible depression will change your life.

## What do Depressions do?

Depressions are not just states of mind or periods of being down. Depressions can be times for one to reconfigure one's own identity, from the bottom up. By being alone and out of sight, we can remove the noise and reflect on our past without distraction and with focus.

In other words: depression is almost like a process of forced self-reflection in the face of a life that doesn't work for you.

### Example 1

As a teen I became depressed because I started doing badly in school. By being depressed, I was able to ingest the new information effectively and in a timely way. As a result of the time alone with a lot of introspection, I was able to figure out why I felt the way I did, and find better recourses.

### Example 2

A few years ago, I was broken up with by a lovely woman in a short rebound relationship. By being subsequently depressed, I was able to reflect on why she would or wouldn't want to be with me, and start taking actions such that I would be appealing to a partner in the future.

### Example 3

I recently went through a total mental breakdown. I was nauseous for days. My brain was buzzing. I couldn't stop crying. I realized my life isn't working for me and it needs to change. By going through the prior depression I was able to dive into the darkest parts of my soul and discover what matters to me (family, friendship, connection, purpose).

## Good vs. Bad Depression

Primarily, good depressions focus on changes to either:

1. **the internal experience** → e.g. changing one's expectations and world view; separating oneself from bad friends
1. **the external experience** → e.g. moving cities; losing weight

In general, bad depressions occur when a person does not change their internal nor external world.

Here are two hypothetical situations and whether depression could be a force for good:

1. "I got laid off by a corporation" → depression is a waste of time. Go find another job. Getting laid off isn't personal: that's just living in 2026.
1. "I got dumped by a partner who is great due to flaws in myself" → depression is an important force, because you need to dig deep and figure it out.

## Why is Depression seen Negatively?

When you have 7 billion people on the planet, an implicit system is constructed, and we are forced to fit in.

In a capitalistic society, that means having to provide to the society to receive in return (I'm not anti-capitalist, just describing facts). The economy and overall system wants you to participate in its system, and it rewards people who help do that: therapists, counselors, psychiatrists, weird gurus, corporate shills and influencers.

Much of depression's symptoms are failing to provide for this society. And society (reasonably) tries to change that.

It's also reasonable for you to resist! I encourage you to at least entertain whether your depression is healthy, not dysfunctional.

## Depressionmaxxing

People are happier to live a shit life than get depressed about it and have to change.

If you want a life that maximizes meaning and understanding in your life: you've got to go deeper. You've got to find the deepest understandings of your experience and the world around you.

Depression helps you go there: cut out the noise, stop listening to the hucksters. Spend time with yourself.

I have found that, when I dive into my depression, I end up much happier having listened to what my soul is trying to tell me: and I start living a more meaningful life as a result!
